{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And he took bread and a skin of water.</b> He did not give silver or gold, because he hated him, for he had gone to bad ways. And further, Abraham had no inheritance to bequeath to him, for he and Ishmael his son were both converts. And it is taught in the first chapter of Kiddushin: a non-Jew does not inherit a convert, nor a convert a convert — neither by Torah law nor by rabbinic law, as it is taught: one who borrowed money from a convert whose sons converted with him and [the convert] died must not repay the sons, and if he repaid them the Sages are not pleased. Nevertheless, his intention was to give him gifts after Sarah's death, as it is written: ״ולבני הפלגשים אשר לאברהם נתן אברהם מתנות״." "<b>He placed on her shoulder.</b> Refers back to ״and a skin of water.״" "<b>And the child.</b> Refers back to Hagar. <b>He placed on her shoulder.</b> For illness had seized him. And if you ask: but they say that until Jacob there was no illness — one may answer: even though at that time there was no illness that kills, nevertheless from thirst they do die, and from this he feared that perhaps he would die of thirst." "<b>And he sent her away.</b> For he had no right to sell her, after he had lain with her, as it is written: ״ושלחתה לנפשה״, and ״מכר לא תמכרנה בכסף״."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:05:12.357933377Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}